WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.520
We are in the beginning of a mass extinction
and all you can talk about is money

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.540
and fairy tales of eternal economic growth,
how dare you!

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:16.520
When Greta Thunberg drew national attention
for her comments at the UN in the Summer of 2019

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:20.280
some praised her performance as a stinging
rebuke to the rich and powerful

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.080
for failing to put the survival of the planet
above their own needs.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.500
At just 16 years old our next guest is already
changing the world-

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.760
She became the biggest voice on the biggest
issue facing the planet.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.960
Others saw the exploitation of a young woman
with emotional problems for propagandist ends.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.580
A mentally ill Swedish child who is being
exploited by her parents and by the international left.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:48.700
But there's no question that Thunberg's style
of environmentalism:

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:55.540
strident, urgent, and critical of global capitalism,
has gained a strong foothold in contemporary politics.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:00.960
A 2019 paper from the journal Biosciences
cosigned by more than eleven thousand scientists

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:07.290
asserted that planet earth's population must
be stabilized and ideally gradually reduced

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.180
and some politicians have questioned the morality
of having children at all.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:16.640
There's scientific consensus that the lives
of children are gonna be very difficult

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.980
and it does lead, I think, young people to
have a legitimate question, you know,

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.480
should, is it ok to still have children?

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:33.000
Educating everyone on the need to curb population
growth seems a reasonable campaign to enact

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.520
would you be courageous enough to discuss
this issue

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.020
and make it a key feature of a plan to address
climate catastrophe.

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:41.530
Well the answer's yes.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:46.720
Fears of overpopulation and ecological disaster
are also beginning to manifest on the far right,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.340
mixed in with an anti-immigrant animus.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:54.210
The logic was expressed in it's most dramatic
and twisted form in the 2019 manifestos

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.759
of mass shooters in both New Zealand and Texas.

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:01.280
If we can get rid of enough people, he wrote,
then out way of life can become more sustainable.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.260
Whether contemporary proponents of these ideas
know it or not,

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:09.259
they're all intellectual errors of the misguided
18th century thinker Robert Thomas Malthus.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:14.820
Who believed that when human population increased,
famine and environmental destruction would ensue.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.980
Malthus argued that population would always
outstrip food supply

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:20.730
because population would grow at exponential
rates

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.220
whereas food supply could only grow at what
he called arithmetic rates.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:29.380
Reason science correspondent Ron Bailey is
the author of the 2015 book End of Doom.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:34.150
He didn't recognize that in fact crops and
livestock are also populations.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:39.310
That they can also be exponentially increased
at the same time as a human population was.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:40.730
And that's exactly what happened.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:44.440
Basically the Malthusian prescription turns
out to be completely wrong.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.099
In the contemporary world, Malthusianism was
most famously expressed

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:54.740
through the work of ecologist Paul Ehrlich,
especially in his 1998 book The Population Bomb.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:59.040
The only hope that there is is that we will
be able, at least in the United States,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.080
through the political process

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.340
to get a government that's courageous enough to say

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.000
'look we'reoverpopulated and we have to have population control

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:06.240
and start moving in that direction.'

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:11.180
He predicted that through the 1970s and 80s
hundreds of millions would starve to death.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.840
He compared humanity to a cancer, writing
that

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.480
"We must shift our efforts from treatment
of the symptoms to the cutting out of the cancer."

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.020
Ehrlich, who still holds an endowed professorship
at Stanford didn't respond to our interview request.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:27.320
His proposed solutions included taxing diapers,
subsidizing vasectomies

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.599
and even spiking food aid and water supplies
with sterilizing drugs

00:03:31.599 --> 00:03:34.990
and then holding a lottery for access to the
antidote.

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:40.880
Similarly ecologist Garret Hardin in 1968
compared humanity to over-breeding cattle.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.740
Writing that 'the freedom to breed is intolerable.'

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.060
The only way to make this system work is to
have the family be willing to give up

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.440
one of its former freedoms

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.700
namely the freedom to determine how many children
it was going to have.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:55.440
Ehrlich would turn out to be as wrongheaded
as Malthus.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.460
Over the next half-century, calories available
per capita steadily increased

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.680
in just about every region of the world

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.640
thanks to improved agricultural techniques
and technology.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.320
Humans are not only consumers, we're also
producers.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:13.400
We're able to create new things, to use resources
in better and better ways over time.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.449
Human creativity can overcome the problems
that Malthusians think that we're going to be suffering

00:04:18.449 --> 00:04:19.709
from over-consumption.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:24.499
We're using fewer and fewer resources to get
more and more value over time.

00:04:24.499 --> 00:04:26.629
And yet world hunger is yet to be eradicated

00:04:26.629 --> 00:04:31.270
with the UN reporting that about ten percent
of the global population is under-nourished

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.900
and perhaps it's true that past trends don't
predict the future.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.740
That's a lot of people, how are we going to
feed them all?

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.689
Karen Pitts who is a member of the Sierra Club
and ran a northern California subcommittee

00:04:41.689 --> 00:04:42.969
on population growth

00:04:42.969 --> 00:04:47.030
is concerned that the world won't be able
to accommodate a population that's expected

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.830
to peak at 11 billion by 2100.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:53.780
She became interested in the topic after a
trip to China in 1996.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:05:00.479
As you flew over the country, every space
was taken up by houses and housing.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:01.990
They are overpopulated.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.659
Whether or not they produce enough food is
a big question and we really can't take the

00:05:06.659 --> 00:05:08.800
risk of being wrong.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.880
While it's true that farmers will have to
become 70% more efficient over the next 30 years

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.879
to feed the growing population,

00:05:15.879 --> 00:05:19.379
the technology already exists to accomplish
that goal.

00:05:19.379 --> 00:05:22.749
If all farmers were as efficient as US corn
growers,

00:05:22.749 --> 00:05:27.160
the world could feed ten billion people today
on half as much land.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.060
And as humanity continues moving into cities,

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.229
the environment will probably be better protected
Bailey points out,

00:05:33.229 --> 00:05:36.760
because this allows for the restoration of
forests and other ecosystems

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:39.320
on the land migrants leave behind.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.659
Something like 90% of people will be living
in cities by the end of the century.

00:05:43.659 --> 00:05:48.719
If that is the case then it'll be less than
2 billion people living on the landscape.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:53.860
Which means that there'll be far more scope
for forests to return, for biodiversity to flourish

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.960
and we'll be using a lot less resources over
that time.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:06:02.309
But today's Malthusians are most concerned
about the disruptive effects of climate change,

00:06:02.309 --> 00:06:05.380
citing global warming, documentarian David Attenborough

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.020
described humanity as a plague upon the earth.

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:13.480
I can't think of a single problem that wouldn't
be easier to solve if there were less people.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:19.420
And the Biosciences paper signed by eleven
thousand scientists projects total societal collapse

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.800
if population isn't managed properly.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:39.560
There's a catastophizing, apocalyptic undercurrent.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.340
Ted Nordhaus, who's a founder of the Breakthrough
Institute,

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:46.120
which advocates technological solutions to
environmental problems,

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:51.180
believes the environmental movement has long
been hindered by its anti-growth paradigm.

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:54.729
Conventional environmental ideology

00:06:54.729 --> 00:07:00.089
posits human development and environmental
protection oppositionally

00:07:00.089 --> 00:07:02.240
and I have exactly the opposite view.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:05.580
Nordhaus says that the most effective way
to deal with climate change

00:07:05.589 --> 00:07:08.909
is by promoting policies that accelerate economic
growth.

00:07:08.909 --> 00:07:13.619
If you're really serious about accelerating
the decline of fertility rates

00:07:13.619 --> 00:07:16.899
and the peak and stabilization of global population,

00:07:16.899 --> 00:07:20.429
you need to accelerate economic development

00:07:20.429 --> 00:07:26.780
for certainly probably 3 or 4 billion people
over the next three or four decades.

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.620
Most of today's environmentalists

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:32.280
don't openly advocate for the draconian population control measures

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.679
pushed by Ehrlich and other Malthusians in
the 1970s.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:38.660
Karen Pitts says that she just wants more
sex education

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:41.700
and greater access to birth control in the developing world.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.940
Pointing to a project she participated in
with Tanzania's local population.

00:07:45.949 --> 00:07:50.159
I have introduced contraception, we put tablets
over there that they could use

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.720
plus being able to administer family planning.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:08:01.559
And the contraception rate went from about
25% to over 54% surprisingly easy.

00:08:01.559 --> 00:08:04.159
Those women wanted family planning.

00:08:04.159 --> 00:08:08.270
Funding greater access to birth control and
education for women in developing countries

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:11.009
was also a recommendation of the Biosciences
paper

00:08:11.009 --> 00:08:16.340
and it's a policy agenda of the UN and leading
NGOs like the Bill and Melinda Gates foundation.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:21.020
Nordhaus says such measures can help with
the margins, but ultimately miss the big picture.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:25.040
Which is that as wealth increases, fertility
rates naturally fall

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.260
as families invest more resources in fewer children.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:34.320
The real drivers of long term fertility decline
and population stabilization around the world

00:08:34.322 --> 00:08:37.629
are just kind of garden variety economic development

00:08:37.629 --> 00:08:42.329
which a lot of the same people signing those
documents are actually saying is the problem.

00:08:42.329 --> 00:08:46.910
The Biosciences paper argues that economic
growth is driving over-consumption of resources

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:51.389
and says 'our goals need to shift from GDP
growth and the pursuit of affluence

00:08:51.389 --> 00:08:53.420
toward sustaining ecosystems...'

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:57.400
Soon as we find new ways to do it our consumption
increases.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.120
That's the problem.

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.040
Pitts is right that people in wealthier societies
tend to consume resources

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:07.280
and generate greenhouse gasses at rates that
are orders of magnitude higher

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.800
than thoseW in the third world.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:11.920
But Nordhaus points out that when poor societies
become wealthy

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:15.640
there are more people positioned to help solve
environmental problems

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:19.100
in the only way that really works: with new
technology.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:23.440
Environmental discourse has been overly focused
on consumption.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.600
Technology is one of the key things that mediates
the relationship

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:32.420
between affluence and consumption and impacts.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.089
Wealthier, more developed societies

00:09:35.089 --> 00:09:40.180
are both better positioned to adapt to problems
like climate change and climate impacts.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:44.550
A category 5 hurricane creates a lot more
devastation

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:50.079
and a lot more loss of life and human impact
in a poorer society than in a rich society.

00:09:50.079 --> 00:09:54.910
They're also better positioned to develop
and deploy new technology.

00:09:54.910 --> 00:10:00.370
Most of the success we've had in deploying
nuclear or other clean energy technologies

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:03.750
is actually in a context where energy demand
is growing quickly.

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:08.580
And so Nordhaus advocates for greater reliance
on clean, abundant energy like nuclear power

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.810
to fuel advanced economies towards possibly
innovating even lower impact alternatives.

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:19.240
But the third world may still need to rely
on traditional fossil fuels on its path to prosperity.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:22.500
Certainly over the next three or four decades
a lot of development,

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.680
particularly in poor countries, is still going
to be fossil based.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:27.780
But it could be natural gas and not coal,

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:32.320
or in Africa, for instance, just there's huge
hydro capacity.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:35.520
In projections of sort of where populations
are going to stabilize

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.980
is really when you get down to the bottom of it

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:42.900
is just ultimately a question of how rapidly
Africa develops economically.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:46.959
Nordhaus says that climate change will likely
continue to present challenges

00:10:46.959 --> 00:10:50.269
for governments, individuals and societies
in the coming decades

00:10:50.269 --> 00:10:55.060
but that it's better to conceive the problem
not as an asteroid hurtling towards earth,

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:58.139
dooming us to extinction unless we thwart
it,

00:10:58.139 --> 00:11:00.949
but as global case of diabetes.

00:11:00.949 --> 00:11:06.089
Diabetes when it's treated is manageable,
it depends on what we do.

00:11:06.089 --> 00:11:08.519
And it's not just about cutting carbon emissions,

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:14.660
it's also development makes us more resilient
to climate extremes of all sorts.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:20.020
Malthus wasn't completely wrong about the
tendency of humans to deplete resources, says Bailey,

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:24.360
but he failed to see that new ways of organizing
society would ameliorate the problem.

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:28.459
Up until a few centuries ago, Robert Thomas
Malthus was about right.

00:11:28.459 --> 00:11:32.740
Is that in fact population was regulated by
food supply.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:37.420
And something changed, the world understood
the role of property rights for example,

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:42.100
the rule of law, and this dramatically changed
the incentive structures that people had.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:47.940
Activists like Naomi Klein who argued that
our economic system is at war with our climate system.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.480
She wants to replace it with some form of
communitarian socialism.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.910
I would suggest to you that doing that would
exactly bring back the Malthusian conditions

00:11:55.910 --> 00:11:57.360
that we used to live in.

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:01.180
The thing that we need to do is proceed to
produce more wealth, more technology

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:06.149
in order to ameliorate, overcome the problems
that climate change is going to cause.

00:12:06.149 --> 00:12:10.300
Her prescription is exactly the opposite of
what needs to be done.

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:16.790
We are in the beginning of a mass extinction
and all you can talk about is money

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:22.069
and fairy tales of eternal economic growth,
how dare you!

00:12:22.069 --> 00:12:26.110
That's the kind of claim that it doesn't actually
enlighten at all.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:30.300
It doesn't actually tell us anything about
the real choices we're faced with.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:36.199
What sorts of social and political and economic
arrangements we ought to aspire to

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:40.720
you know for a planet that is pretty soon
going to be nine or ten billion people.

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:46.339
What it's not is gonna be agrarian, traditional
economies.

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:50.700
With ten billion people if you ever tried
to actually like actually have everybody live that way

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:54.980
we would just cut down every forest in the world
and then we would collapse.

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:57.779
This is not real, these are fantasies.

00:12:57.779 --> 00:13:03.800
She's going to go back to Copenhagen and live
a very righteous life as an international

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:05.980
environmental celebrity

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:10.920
in a wealthy city surrounded by extraordinary
modern infrastructure

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:13.040
most of which was built with fossil fuels.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.839
That's Greta Thunberg's future

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:17.860
and I would like that future for everybody
else on the planet.

